<p>Hello Moksydoesstuff! let's take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: An anomalous friendship bracelet kit (possibly from Dr. Wondertainment) that anomalously links those who wear created friendship bracelets. Effects vary depending on what materials are used in the bracelets, letter beads could allow telepathic communication between the wearers etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Currently the concept seems a bit wishy washy, there isn't any clear effects or much of a purpose presented here. Where's the attention grabber? whats the skips in-universe reason fo existing?- how will said reason/purpose play a role in the narrative?</p>
<p>As it stands the concept doesn't really show much other than effects, while there are small hints that the bracelet was made by Wondertainment, there isn't anything shown here (or in the narrative) that expands upon that.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The central narrative is told through several diary entries and notes left between the two previous owners. The story is about two close friends who drift apart, despite being anomalously linked. Diary entries will be from the perspective of Angela, who is doing everything she can to not lose Bri (her best friend) who moved away. Bri slowly pulls away and starts going down a different path than her friend. Angela knows this, but still fights to keep her best friend. This proved unsuccessful in the end however, as Bri suddenly cut ties with Angela, evidently removing the bracelet and all other methods of communication. About a month after this, Angela receives a letter along with Bri's bracelet and half of the kit.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall I'm unsure on how the story will fit into an article format, the Foundation doesn't really get a mention and the whole narrative/concept seems dependant on the journal entires (which alone won't fit well into an SCP article format).</p>
<p>Along with this, the characters aren't particularly attention grabbing, as there isn't anything to really get invested in.- why should I as a reader care about these two characters? what makes them interesting? who are they? what are their motives? why is Angela so set on continuing the friendship?- does it have something to do with the brackets effects? is the Foundation involved with it?</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>-I would recommend adding the Foundation into the narrative, give them some sort of role, wether they're involved during these events or after.- do note that the article is written from their perspective, with what they've learnt from observing the skip and/or the people its affected.</p>
<p>-I would recommend adding in a purpose, currently the bracelet doesn't really have any reason to exist, which makes the skip feel a bit flat.- its much harder to write something that doesn't have a purpose as the object or person doesn't have any overarching goals, nothing to uphold or achieve. As a result, the narrative and concept can be much harder to get invested in.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>-RED</p>

